</div>', '<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #001d35; background: white;">R P &Aacute;frica: Portuguese public television channel tailored to Lusophone African countries. It is available free-to-air via the country&rsquo;s digital terrestrial television network (unlike in most other countries where it''s cable or satellite-only).</span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #001d35; background: white;"><span style="mso-spacerun: yes;">&nbsp;</span>TVI Internacional: Portuguese television channel offering a blend of telenovelas, news, and entertainment for global Portuguese-speaking audiences. It is available in Cape Verde as part of its international reach across Africa, Europe, North America, Latin America, and more.</span></p>', '<p>Online media in Cape Verde is made up of legacy media websites and individual content makers</p>', '<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 0in; line-height: 16.5pt; background: white;"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family: '' imes New Roman''; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #545d7e; letter-spacing: .1pt; mso-font-kerning: 0pt; mso-ligatures: none; mso-fareast-language: EN-GB;">The internet is generally not restricted in Cape Verde, and there are no reports of government monitoring of email or internet access. The online media landscape offers a mixture of state-run and private media online. It includes a state news agency (<em>Inforpress</em>), around two privately-owned newspapers, and about five standalone news websites.</span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 0in; line-height: 16.5pt; background: white;"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family: ''Times New Roman''; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #545d7e; letter-spacing: .1pt; mso-font-kerning: 0pt; mso-ligatures: none; mso-fareast-language: EN-GB;">&nbsp;</span></p>', '<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;">Leading media include <a href="http://www.anacao.cv/"><span lang="P -BR" style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white; mso-ansi-language: PT-BR;">A Nacao,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://expressodasilhas.cv/"><span lang="PT-BR" style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white; mso-ansi-language: PT-BR;">Expresso das Ilhas,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="http://www.asemana.publ.cv/"><span lang="PT-BR" style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white; mso-ansi-language: PT-BR;">A Semana,&nbsp;</span></a><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #001d35; background: white; mso-ansi-language: PT-BR;"> </span><a href="http://www.rtc.cv/"><span lang="PT-BR" style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white; mso-ansi-language: PT-BR;">TCV,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="http://www.rtc.cv/"><span lang="PT-BR" style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white; mso-ansi-language: PT-BR;">RCV,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://www.facebook.com/RNM.RadioNovadeMaria/"><span lang="PT-BR" style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white; mso-ansi-language: PT-BR;">Radio Nova de Maria,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://liveonlineradio.net/fr/radio-comercial-cape-verde"><span lang="PT-BR" style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white; mso-ansi-language: PT-BR;">Radio Comercial,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://praiafm.biz/"><span lang="PT-BR" style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white; mso-ansi-language: PT-BR;">Praia FM</span><strong><span lang="PT-BR" style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white; mso-ansi-language: PT-BR;">.&nbsp;</span></strong></a></p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2025-12-09 14:52:25.90521'),
(6, 6, '<p><span lang="EN-US">Media freedom in Cote d&rsquo;Ivoire is guaranteed by the </span><a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Cote_DIvoire_2016.pdf"><span lang="EN-US">2016 Constitution</span></a> <span lang="EN-US">through Article 19 which provisions for free expression and Article 18 guarantees the right to information.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Law No 2017-868 specifically regulates the media industry. Also, Law No 2017-868 (Article 89) excludes detention and imprisonment for offenses committed by the press. However, the law still contains provisions that violate the right to freedom of expression as it prescribes excessive fines for:&nbsp; Insulting the Head of State (up to CFA Francs 10 million (about US$17,000); Publication, dissemination; disclosure or reproduction of false news; defamation against courts, the armed forces, members of the government and the National Assembly: up to CFA Francs 5 million. In the case of press offenses committed against the Head of State, the public prosecutor of the Republic may institute proceedings without prior complaint of the victim (Article 39).</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>In 2024 Parliament passed the electronics communication law which contains the controversial article 214 which says: &lsquo;&rsquo;anyone who intercepts, discloses, publishes or uses the content of messages or reveals their existence&rdquo; could be sentenced to five years in prison and a fine of 10 million CFA francs (around 15,245 euros).</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Articles 40-63 of the 2017 law created the Autorite National de la Presse (ANP) to replace the Conseil National de la Presse (CNP) as the media regulatory body responsible for enforcing the professional code of the media.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>C&ocirc;te d&rsquo;Ivoire does not currently criminalise defamation/libel with imprisonment under its press law, but it <em data-start="118" data-end="124">does</em> retain sanctions for defamation that are penal (fines) under its legal framework. &nbsp; Under C&ocirc;te d&rsquo;Ivoire&rsquo;s 2017 Press Law ((Loi n&deg; 867 de 2017), defamation and related press offences are subject to fines, not imprisonment<strong data-start="435" data-end="473">.</strong></p>', '<p> he <a href="https://anp.ci/">Autorite Nationale de la Presse (ANP)</a> which regulates print and online media while <a href="https://www.haca.ci/">High Authority for Audiovisual Communication (HACA)</a> regulates television and radio.&nbsp;</p>', '<p><a href="https://carte-unjci.org/">Union Nationale des Journalistes de C&ocirc;te d''Ivoire (UNJCI)</a>is the main journalists Union in Cote d&rsquo;Ivoire. &nbsp;Others are:</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;Syndicat National des Professionnels de la Presse de C&ocirc;te d''Ivoire (SYNAPPCI),</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Observatoire de la Libert&eacute; de la Presse, de l''Ethique et de la D&eacute;ontologie (<a href="http://www.cijp.communication.gouv.ci/accueil/journaliste/1">OLPED</a>),</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Organisation des Journalistes Professionnels de C&ocirc;te d''Ivoire (<a href="http://www.cijp.communication.gouv.ci/accueil/journaliste/1">OJPCI</a>)</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Union Nationale des Journalistes de C&ocirc;te d&rsquo;Ivoire (<a href="http://www.cijp.communication.gouv.ci/accueil/journaliste/1">UNJCI</a>),</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;Groupement des Editeurs de Presse de C&ocirc;te d&rsquo;Ivoire (<a href="http://www.cijp.communication.gouv.ci/accueil/journaliste/1">GEPCI</a>)</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;Conf&eacute;d&eacute;ration Nationale des Directeurs de Publication de C&ocirc;te d&rsquo;Ivoire (<a href="http://www.cijp.communication.gouv.ci/accueil/journaliste/1">CNDPCI</a>)</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>R&eacute;seau des Professionnels de la Presse en Ligne de C&ocirc;te d&rsquo;Ivoire (<a href="http://www.cijp.communication.gouv.ci/accueil/journaliste/1">REPPRELCI</a></p>', '<p> he HACA&rsquo;s directory for radio stations <a href="https://www.haca.ci/annuaires/repertoire-des-operateurs-radio">list 400 channels</a> as officially registered radio stations.</p>', '<p> he HACA&rsquo;s directory for TV lists <a href="https://www.haca.ci/annuaires/repertoire-des-operateurs-tele">7 channels</a> as the officially registered TV stations.</p>', '<p> here are more than 100 newspapers in Cote d&rsquo;Ivoire.</p>', '<p><a href="http://www.rti.ci/">Radiodiffusion  elevision Ivoirienne (RTI),&nbsp;</a>&nbsp;is state-run audiovisual broadcaster. It operates two national networks - &nbsp;La Premi&egrave;re (RTI 1), which broadcasts nationally, and TV2 (RTI 2). <a href="http://www.fratmat.info/">Fraternit&eacute; Matin,&nbsp;external</a>&nbsp;is the&nbsp; state-owned daily newspaper, <a title="Radiodiffusion Television Ivoirienne" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radiodiffusion_Television_Ivoirienne">Radiodiffusion Television Ivoirienne</a>&nbsp;(RTI).</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a title="La Premi&egrave;re (RTI)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Premi%C3%A8re_(RTI)">La Premi&egrave;re</a>, <a title="RTI 2" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RTI_2">RTI 2</a>, <a title="La 3 (page does not exist)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=La_3&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1">La 3</a>, <a title="RTI Sport TV (page does not exist)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=RTI_Sport_TV&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1">RTI Sport TV</a>, <a title="RTI Music TV (page does not exist)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=RTI_Music_TV&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1">RTI Music TV</a>, <a title="La Nouvelle Cha&icirc;ne Ivoirienne (page does not exist)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=La_Nouvelle_Cha%C3%AEne_Ivoirienne&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1">La Nouvelle Cha&icirc;ne Ivoirienne</a>&nbsp;(NCI), T&eacute;l&eacute;vision Ivory Coast (TCI), <a title="TV2 (C&ocirc;te d''Ivoire) (page does not exist)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=TV2_(C%C3%B4te_d%27Ivoire)&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1">TV2</a>, <a title="TAM-TAM TV (page does not exist)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=TAM-TAM_TV&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1">TAM-TAM TV</a>, <a title="Africahit Music TV (page does not exist)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Africahit_Music_TV&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1">Africahit Music TV</a>.</p>', '<p>While <a href="http://www.aip.ci/">Agence Ivoirienne de Presse (AIP),</a> is the national news agency</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>', '<p><a title="Canal+ Afrique" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canal%2B_Afrique">Canal+ Afrique</a>, formerly Canal+ Horizons, is the sole private television broadcaster to transmit on the hertz network in&nbsp;<a title="Abidjan" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abidjan">Abidjan</a>. It is a project of the French-owned&nbsp;<a title="Groupe Canal+" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groupe_Canal%2B">Groupe Canal+</a>, and targets a French-speaking African audience.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a title=" V5MONDE" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TV5MONDE">TV5MONDE</a>, is French-owned international broadcaster that reaches countries throughout "la francophonie" (the French-speaking world), is offered to more subscribers than Canal+ Horizons. This is in spite of the fact that it could be accessed directly like CFI-TV, another satellite station.&nbsp;<a title="Canal+ (French TV channel)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canal%2B_(French_TV_channel)">Canal+</a>&nbsp;Vert is also available by subscribe.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>"Le Sat", is a satellite TV service provider owned by the former Sofirad. It can be picked up in Abidjan with MMDS antenna&nbsp;:&nbsp;<a title="TV5 Afrique (page does not exist)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=TV5_Afrique&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1">TV5 Afrique</a>,&nbsp;<a title="RTL9" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RTL9">RTL9</a>,&nbsp;<a title="Festival" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Festival">Festival</a>,&nbsp;<a title="TiJi" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TiJi">TiJi</a>,&nbsp;<a title="Mangas" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mangas">Mangas</a>,&nbsp;<a title="Trace TV" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trace_TV">MCM Africa</a>, a subsidiary of MCM), Euronews, Plan&egrave;te, etc.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>There are three foreign radio broadcasters that can be received in Ivory Coast on the FM band: RFI (Abidjan and&nbsp;<a title="Korhogo" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korhogo">Korhogo</a>), Africa N&deg;1 (Abidjan) and BBC (Abidjan). Voice of America (VOA) obtained a license in 2001 with a view to transmitting in FM in Abidjan</p>', '<p> he HACA lists <a href="https://www.haca.ci/annuaires/repertoire-des-web-tv">7 channels</a> in its directory for online television media and <a href="https://www.haca.ci/annuaires/repertoire-des-web-radios">10 channels</a> for online radio. There however are several websites and blogs that serve news to the public. <a href="https://www.einpresswire.com/world-media-directory/3/cote-d%27ivoire">See here</a>.</p>', '<p>Internet freedom is generally considered to be good, with no credible reports of government restrictions on access or censorship of content.&nbsp;Also, there are no credible reports of the government mounting surveillance on internet users.</p>', '<p class="ssrcss-1q0x1qg-paragraph"><a href="http://www.communication.gouv.ci/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Minist&egrave;re de la Communication&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">, </span></strong><a href="http://www.fratmat.info/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Fraternite Matin&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">, </span></strong><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Soir Info&nbsp;</span></strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">, </span><a href="http://www.lenouveaureveil.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Le Nouveau Reveil&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">, </span></strong><a href="http://www.linfodrome.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">L&rsquo;Inter&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">,</span></strong><a href="http://www.notrevoie.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Notre Voie&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">, </span></strong><a href="http://www.imatin.net/journal/quotidiens/le-quotidien-d-abidjan/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Le Quotidien d&rsquo;Abidjan,</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;"> </span></strong><a href="http://www.lepatriote.net/main/lejournal.aspx" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Le Patriote&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">,</span></strong><a href="http://www.nordsudquotidien.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Nord-Sud Quotidien,</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;"> </span></strong><a href="http://nouveaucourrier.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Le Nouveau Courrier&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">, </span></strong><a href="http://www.imatin.net/journal/quotidiens/l-expression/5835_2013-06-13.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">L&rsquo;Expression&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">, </span></strong><a href="http://www.lintelligentdabidjan.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">L&rsquo;Intelligent d&rsquo;Abidjan&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">, </span></strong><a title="http://urpci.net " href="http://urpci.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">URPCI&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">, </span></strong><a href="http://www.aip.ci/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Agence Ivoirienne de Presse, </span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;"><span style="mso-spacerun: yes;">&nbsp;</span></span></strong><a href="http://www.onucifm.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">ONUCI FM, </span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;"><span style="mso-spacerun: yes;">&nbsp;</span></span></strong><a href="http://www.rti.ci/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">R I Television&nbsp;</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">,</span></strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #202122;"> </span><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">Radio Yopougon (community radio), Radio ATM Port-Bou&euml;t (community radio), City FM (private license holder, Treichville), Radio Abidjan 1 (ex RFS) (private license holder, Cocody), Albayane FM, Cocody FM (private license holder, Cocody), Radio Alobh&eacute; Bingerville, 100.8 FM (Radio Communale), Radio Espoir (religious non-commercial station), Z&eacute;nith FM, Radio Amiti&eacute;, Radio Anyama, Radio Nostalgie, part of Nostalgie International, received in Abidjan<strong><sup>, </sup></strong></span><a title="Radio Jam (page does not exist)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Radio_Jam&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">JAM FM</span></strong></a><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">.&nbsp;</span></strong></p>
<p class="ssrcss-1q0x1qg-paragraph"><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">&nbsp;</span></strong></p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2026-01-29 13:13:40.887156'),
(7, 2, '<p><span lang="EN-US"> he 1992 constitution is the primary guarantor of media freedom through </span><a href="https://judicial.gov.gh/index.php/freedom-and-independence-of-the-media"><span lang="EN-US">Chapter 12</span></a> <span lang="EN-US">of the document. Ghana has a </span><a href="https://ohcs.gov.gh/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Right-to-Information-Act-2019-Act-989-2.pdf"><span lang="EN-US">right to information law</span></a> <span lang="EN-US">with the </span><a href="https://rtic.gov.gh/"><span lang="EN-US">Right To Information Commission</span></a> <span lang="EN-US">as the custodian agency for the operationalization of the law and compliance enforcement. </span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="EN-US">The country does not directly harbor criminal libel laws after it was </span><a href="https://ghalii.org/akn/gh/act/2001/602/eng@2001-08-17"><span lang="EN-US">repealed</span></a> <span lang="EN-US">in 2001. However, Ghana&rsquo;s statutes are fraught with </span>Section 208 of <a href="https://repository.parliament.gh/bitstream/handle/123456789/2434/ACT%2029.pdf?sequence=1&amp;isAllowed=y">the Criminal and Offences Act 1960 (Act 29)</a> and the Electronic Communications Act (Act 775) - two archaic laws that hide in plain sight as <a href="https://mfwa.org/issues-in-focus/the-two-laws-surviving-the-repealed-criminal-libel-law-in-ghana/">alter-ego</a> of the repealed criminal libel law.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #001d35; background: white;">Ghana also has a <a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/ir.parliament.gh/bitstream/handle/123456789/1800/CYBERSECURITYACT,2020(ACT1038).pdf?sequence=1">Cybersecurity Act, 2020 (Act 1038),</a> which established the <a href="https://moc.gov.gh/csa/">Cyber Security Authority (CSA</a>) to regulate digital security, promote cybersecurity, and tackle cybercrime, covering offenses like non-consensual sharing of intimate images, with penalties including jail time, while a proposed 2025 Amendment Bill seeks to give the CSA more prosecutorial powers and clarify enforcement.</span></p>', '<p> he <a href="https://www.nmc.org.gh/">National Media Commission</a> is the main media regulator. It facilitates the registration of media houses, mediate and resolves media-related complaints and provides policy advisory on the media.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="https://moc.gov.gh/nca/#:~:text=NCA%20promotes%20fair%20competition%20and,practices%20by%20other%20service%20providers.">National Communications Authority (NCA)</a> is the statutory body mandated to&nbsp;license and regulate electronic communications activities and services.</p>', '<p> he <a href="https://www.gjaghana.org/">Ghana Journalists Association (GJA)</a> is the main umbrella body for journalists in the country, with its membership cutting across radio, television, newspaper and new media journalists.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;The <a href="https://prinpag.com/">Private Newspapers and Online Publishers Association (PRINPAG)</a> is an association for newspaper and online news publishers.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.gibagh.org/">Ghana Independent Broadcasters Association (GIBA)</a> is the umbrella body for all duly authorized privately-owned and operational radio and television broadcast organisations in Ghana.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="https://swagghana.com/">Sports Writers Association of Ghana (SWAG)</a> is the umbrella body for sports journalists in Ghana.</p>', '<p><a href="https://nca.org.gh/authorised-radio/#:~:text= he%20National%20Communications%20Authority%20(NCA,as%20at%20Fourth%20Quarter%2C%202023.">747 authorized FM Broadcasting Stations with 550 operational</a>.</p>', '<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://nca.org.gh/television/#:~:text=As%20at%20the%20end%20of, elevision%20Programme%20Channel%20(Nationwide%20Coverage)">175 authorized TV stations, with1 Analogue&nbsp;Terrestrial Television. 42&nbsp;Digital Terrestrial&nbsp;Free-To-Air Television Programme Channel (Nationwide Coverage)</a></p>', '<p>According to the National Media Commission (NMC) since 2023, it has registered 70 newspapers.</p>', '<p><a href="https://www.gbcghanaonline.com/">Ghana Broadcasting Corporation (GBC)</a> is the only state broadcaster. It operates some 25 media channels across the country including 17 radio stations broadcasting in 23 languages, six television channels with 31 transmission sites.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;<a href="https://corporate.graphic.com.gh/brands.html">Graphic Communications Group Limited (GCGL)</a> is one of two main newspaper publishers owned by the state, Graphic publishes a variety of newspapers, including national broadsheet, the Daily Graphic, Junior Graphic,  he Mirror, Graphic Business, Graphic Sports, and Graphic Showbiz.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://newtimescorporation.com.gh/about/">The New Times Corporation</a> is the second state-owned newspaper publisher with the <a href="https://ghanaiantimes.com.gh/">Ghanaian Times newspaper</a> and <a href="https://thespectatoronline.com/">The Spectator</a> as its flagship titles.</p>', '<p><a href="https://gna.org.gh/">Ghana News Agency (GNA)</a> is the national newswire service owned and operated by the state.</p>', '<p>Several international media organizations have a presence or operate projects in Ghana, including&nbsp;<a href="https://www.google.com/search?rlz=1C1GCEJ_enGH1021GH1021&amp;cs=0&amp;sca_esv=1c2c48b035966a68&amp;sxsrf=AE3 ifM8yBA0oo3XD8oBYpmd2r9Z6QTYkw%3A1755698591504&amp;q=Deutsche+Welle+%28DW%29&amp;sa=X&amp;ved=2ahUKEwjF4OT2xpmPAxVHTUEAHVONAEUQxccNegQIAhAB&amp;mstk=AUtExfA8XNw1oSfu9eVTkcPwVNf4OJmLA7ua7uCuIpU-WQQtfnG9lDwT87OxJcXZMzNG3grdKAf3OIW5U3LPyjbtjAcOu32wT6DMPXspZiP62MGtwMCUHBkfzzc_Pl5lYYbhFzz7BJiE8Ea4ssSKDtAJv9elQhQ-xSoH7xxLvc_nzkKvVpE&amp;csui=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deutsche Welle (DW)</a>&nbsp;Akademie, and BBC Monitoring.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.dstv.com/en-gh/about/dstv-ghana/">DSTV Ghana</a> is privately owned, mostly entertainment-focused paid-television channel, operated by Multichoice Ghana Limited, a venture of Multichoice Africa.</p>', '<p>Online media form a prominent part of Ghana&rsquo;s press culture with the online media landscape mostly made up of websites of legacy media organizations, but with dedicated news websites and news portals also in the mix.</p>', '<p> he state of internet freedom in Ghana is <a href="https://freedomhouse.org/country/ghana/freedom-net/2024">generally regarded as safe</a>. There is no known shutdown or interference with the internet, neither is there any evidence that the government does surveillance of users of the internet.</p>', '<p>Peace FM, Joy FM, G V, GBC Radio, Adom FM, TV 3, Joy News, Metro TV, Okay FM, Daily Guide newspaper.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>For online media, there are <a href="https://www.myjoyonline.com/">myjoyonline.com</a>, <a href="https://www.gbcghanaonline.com/">gbcghanaonline.com</a>, <a href="https://www.peacefmonline.com/">peacefmonline.com</a>, <a href="https://citifmonline.com/">citifmonline.com</a>, <a href="https://3news.com/live/tv3/">3news.com</a>, <a href="https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/SportsArchive/">Ghanaweb.com</a>, <a href="https://www.modernghana.com/">modernghana.com</a> and <a href="https://thefourthestategh.com/">thefourthestategh.com</a></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>(<a href="https://www.mom-gmr.org/en/countries/ghana/">more here</a>)</p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2025-12-16 11:32:23.885976'),
(8, 8, '<p><span lang="EN-US"> he primary guarantor of media freedom is the 1996 Constitution through </span><a href="http://biblioteka.sejm.gov.pl/wp-content/uploads/2017/04/Gwinea_Bissau_port_010117.pdf">&nbsp;Article 56. &nbsp;</a>&nbsp;Specifically however, the media in the country is governed by a press law, with &nbsp;<a href="https://www.didinho.org/Arquivo/Lei%20da%20Imprensa.htm">Article 5 of the Press Law, 1991,</a> specifying the right to freely own news media organization. <span lang="EN-US">The country also has a Press Freedom Law (2013), Article 4 of which gives recognition to the fundamental rights and freedoms of journalists. This same Article guarantees the right to freely print and distribute publications.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">Guinea Bissau&rsquo;s <a href="https://ihl-databases.icrc.org/en/national-practice/penal-code-and-code-criminal-procedure-1993">Penal Code</a> (Criminal Code) &ndash; Articles 126 &amp; 127<strong>, </strong>criminalizes defamation and libel. <span style="mso-spacerun: yes;">&nbsp;</span>Article 126 criminalises defamatory and insulting statements made publicly or via communication channels. It penalises anyone who, without justification, imputes to another person a fact or expresses an opinion that harms their honour or reputation, with imprisonment of up to 1 year or a fine.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">Article 127 increases penalties (e.g., up to about 18 months) if the offence is committed by<strong> </strong>a media outlet or against someone performing public, religious, or political functions.</p>
<p><span lang="EN-US">&nbsp;</span></p>
<p><span lang="EN-US">Guinea Bissau does not have a specific law on Access to Information, however, the Press Law, through </span>Article 9<span lang="EN-US"> (</span>4/91<span lang="EN-US">) makes</span><span lang="EN-US"> generic provision for journalists to be allowed access official information on demand even though in practice, this access is not truly guaranteed.</span></p>', '<p> he media environment is regulated by three main bodies: <a href="https://web.facebook.com/usvpp.guineabissau/photos/agradecemos-ao-ministro-da-comunica%C3%A7%C3%A3o-social-dr-florentino-f-dias-pela-calorosa/1092443866252783/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Minist&eacute;rio da Comunica&ccedil;&atilde;o Social </a>&nbsp;<span lang="EN-US">(Ministry of Social Communication)</span>,<span lang="EN-US"> which</span> primar<span lang="EN-US">ily</span> plan<span lang="EN-US">s</span>, coordinate and execute social communication policies. It also protects journalists and communication service providers from political interference in order to guarantee press freedom and editorial autonomy and assists the state and other authorities in matters concerning the media.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;<a href="https://arn.gw/activeapp/">Autoridade Nacional Reguladora</a> (national regulatory authority), is responsible for: ensuring the exercise of the right to information and freedom of the press; independence of the media from political and economic powers; independence and pluralism <span lang="EN-US">among</span> state media organisation<span lang="EN-US">s</span>; guaranteeing the rights to airtime,<span lang="EN-US"> and;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.didinho.org/Arquivo/ledoconselhonacionaldacomunicacaosocial.htm">Conselho Nacional da Comunica&ccedil;&atilde;o Social</a> (National Media Council), <span lang="EN-US">which is the</span> entity responsible for regulating the telecommunications and broadcasting sector in the country.&nbsp; It ensures that operations are conducted legally and transparently. This may include granting operating licenses to media companies, monitoring compliance with content and advertising standards, protecting the rights of communication services consumers, and promoting healthy competition in the media market. Additionally, the ARN is involved in developing policies and strategies to promote equitable access to information and communication technology throughout society.</p>', '<p><span lang="P -BR">Media Associations in Guinea Bissau include Sindicato dos </span><a href="https://mediagb.org/sinjotecs/"><span lang="PT-BR">Jornalistas e T&eacute;cnicos de Comunica&ccedil;&atilde;o Social</span></a><span lang="PT-BR"> (SINJOTECS - Union of Journalists and Technicians),&nbsp;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://renarc.gw/renarc/apresentacao"><span lang="PT-BR">Rede Nacional das R&aacute;dios e Televis&otilde;es Comunit&aacute;rias</span></a><span lang="PT-BR"> (RENARC-Association of Community Radio and Television),</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://web.facebook.com/profile.php?id=100057932748643"><span lang="PT-BR">Ordem dos Jornalistas da Guin&eacute;-Bissau</span></a><span lang="PT-BR"> (OJGB - Association of Guinea Bissau Journalists), and</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://web.facebook.com/amcsmocambique/?locale=pt_BR&amp;_rdc=1&amp;_rdr"><span lang="PT-BR">Associa&ccedil;&atilde;o das Mulheres Profissionais da Comunica&ccedil;&atilde;o Social</span></a> <span lang="PT-BR">(AMPROCS - Association of Women Journalists).&nbsp; </span></p>', '<p>Guinea Bissau has about 41 radio stations, being a mix of private and community broadcasters.</p>', '<p>Guinea Bissau has 4  V stations.</p>', '<p> here are at least 14 newspapers and magazines In Guinea Bissau.</p>', '<p><span lang="EN-US">Guinea Bissau has </span>&nbsp;4 (four) state-owned media, including national television, radio station, newspaper, and the <span lang="EN-US">national newswire service (news</span> agency<span lang="EN-US">)</span>.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;<a href="https://rdngbissau.gw/">Radiodifusao Nacional da Guin&eacute;-Bissau (RDN)</a>, <span lang="EN-US">is </span>the national radio <span lang="EN-US">broadcaster. </span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.facebook.com/tgbecp24/?locale=pt_BR"> elevis&atilde;o da Guin&eacute;-Bissau (TGB)</a> <span lang="EN-US">I the national television broadcaster.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="EN-US">Guinea Bissau also has state-run newspapers, including,</span> <a href="http://jornalnopintcha.gw">Jornal N&ocirc; Pintcha</a><span lang="EN-US">,</span> is the public newspaper created by the government of Guinea<span lang="EN-US">.</span></p>', '<p><a href="https://ang.gw"><span lang="P -BR">Ag&ecirc;ncia de Not&iacute;cias da Guin&eacute;, (ANG</span></a><span lang="PT-BR">)</span><span lang="PT-BR"> &nbsp;the country&rsquo;s press agency.</span></p>', '<p> hree international broadcast media organizations have outlets in Guinea Bissau. They are:</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://rtpafrica.rtp.pt/cultura/guine-bissau/#google_vignette">RTP &Aacute;frica</a>,</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://rdpafrica.rtp.pt/#google_vignette">RDP &Aacute;frica</a>,</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.lusa.pt/lusofonia/guine-bissau">LUSA</a> &nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>', '<p>Guinea Bissau has some 22 online media outlets.</p>', '<p>Internet freedom in Guinea-Bissau is generally not restricted by the government, with no apparent censorship or restrictions on online content.&nbsp;Internet shutdown is not the norm, with the country recording its first internet shutdown in 2024 triggered by protests and political instability.</p>', '<p><span lang="P -BR">R&aacute;dio Gal&aacute;xia de Pindjiquiti, R&aacute;dio, Bombolom, R&aacute;dio Sol Mansi, R&aacute;dio Jovem, R&aacute;dio Nossa, R&aacute;dio Luz FM, R&aacute;dio Cidade FM, R&aacute;dio Paz, R&aacute;dio Sensibiliza&ccedil;&atilde;o, R&aacute;dio Capital FM, R&aacute;dio &Aacute;frica FM, R&aacute;dio Evang&eacute;lica Voz da Esperan&ccedil;a, R&aacute;dio Escola S&atilde;o Jos&eacute;, R&aacute;dio Al-Barakah, List of Community Radios, R&aacute;dio Kassumai, R&aacute;dio Uler Abaand, R&aacute;dio Rio Cacheu, R&aacute;dio Titidjene, R&aacute;dio EVA, R&aacute;dio Comunit&aacute;ria Bigene, R&aacute;dio Balofom, R&aacute;dio Comunit&aacute;ria de Bafat&aacute;, R&aacute;dio Mulher de Bafat&aacute;, R&aacute;dio Sintcham OCCO, R&aacute;dio Gandal , R&aacute;dio Leste FM, R&aacute;dio Evang&eacute;lica, R&aacute;dio Colinas de Bo&eacute;, R&aacute;dio Voz de Pitche, R&aacute;dio Sancorla , R&aacute;dio Esperan&ccedil;a Bolama, R&aacute;dio Bijag&oacute;s, R&aacute;dio Djan-Djan, R&aacute;dio Voz de UROK, R&aacute;dio Kossena R&aacute;dio Okinka Pampa, R&aacute;dio Lamparam, R&aacute;dio Papagaio, R&aacute;dio Educativa de Cati&oacute;, R&aacute;dio Voz de Paz, R&aacute;dio Djalicunda, R&aacute;dio Ndj&eacute;repa C&oacute;, R&aacute;dio Nova, R&aacute;dio Voz de Quelel&eacute;, R&aacute;dio Evang&eacute;lica, Registered Community Television channels, TV Comunit&aacute;ria de Quelel&eacute;, TV Comunit&aacute;ria Pkis Utchak, TV Mansar, TV Bagunda, List of Digital Media Platforms, R&aacute;dio Bantaba, Bissau Online, Capital News, Guin&eacute;-Bissau DW, GNB Di&aacute;rio de Not&iacute;cias, Jornal N&ocirc; Pintcha, Jornal O Democrata, Portal Guigui, Lusa, ANG, O Golo GB, TV Obulum, Donos da Bola, Ditadura e Consenso, Fut 245, CAP-GB, Canal Djurtus, N&ocirc; sta Djunto, R&aacute;dio Jovem , &Uacute;ltima Hora, R&aacute;dio Novo Tempo, Donos da Bola , RTP &Aacute;frica, RDP &Aacute;frica, LUSA&nbsp; </span></p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2026-01-29 13:16:41.818627'),
(9, 9, '<p>Free expression and press freedom are guaranteed by the <a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/adsdatabase.ohchr.org/IssueLibrary/GUINEA_Constitution.pdf">2010 Constitution</a>, specifically, through Section 46.&nbsp;Guinea also has a press code which is replete with provisions that criminalizes media work. Among others, the Code stipulates that freedom of expression can be restricted by the necessity to respect human dignity, maintain public order, and safeguard national security.&nbsp; Article 51 compels reporters to reveal sources if asked to do so by the state prosecutor&rsquo;s office. Per the 1991 Press Code, truth is no defence in libel cases if the facts relate to a defendant&rsquo;s private life.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Under Article 14, three copies of each edition of every newspaper must be submitted to the legal, administrative, and judiciary authorities four hours before distribution. Failure to submit copies within the prescribed time is punishable by conviction to a fine of US$500.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Defamation is a criminal offense in Guinea. A criminal Code &ndash; <a href="https://ihl-databases.icrc.org/en/national-practice/law-criminal-code-2016">Loi No. 2016-059/AN portant Code P&eacute;nal</a> (adopted in 2016) retained penalties of up to five years in prison for defamation or insult of public figures. Journalists found guilty of crimes against state security can be jailed for up to five years. Defamation of the head of state, for which lawsuits can be filed by the state prosecutor without presidential consent, carries a one-year prison sentence and a US$1,200 fine. Defamation of the judiciary, the Army, members of parliament, government and public administration officials is punishable by a one-year prison term.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Under Article 26, publishers face sedition charges if they fail to disclose the production and distribution schedule of their newspapers to the authorities at the start of every electoral campaign. Journalists accused of defamation during an election period must respond to court summonses within 24 hours.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>', '<p> he High Authority for Communication (HAC) is the main media regulator.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.arpt.gov.gn/">Autorit&eacute; de R&eacute;gulation des Postes et T&eacute;l&eacute;communications (ARPT)</a> regulates the telecommunications sector.</p>', '<p class="MsoNormal"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">Journalists Associations include </span><a href="https://web.facebook.com/agepiguinee.agepiguinee/?locale=lt_L &amp;_rdc=1&amp;_rdr"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Guin&eacute;enne des &eacute;diteurs de la Presse Ind&eacute;pendante (AGEPI)</span></a><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;"> </span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">(Guinea''s independent newspaper publishers'' association) which focuses on the interests of </span><span style="color: rgb(20, 20, 20);">independent newspaper publishers. </span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a href="https://web.facebook.com/people/Syndicat-des-Professionnels-de-la-Presse-de-Guin%C3%A9e/100079450399942/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white;">Syndicat des professionnels de la presse de Guinee (SPPG)</span></a><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #001d35; background: white;"> </span><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #141414;">is focused on improving the working conditions of journalists in Guinea.&nbsp;</span></p>', '<p>Guinea has more than 80 private, state-owned and community <a href="https://worldradiomap.com/gn/conakry">radio stations</a>.</p>', '<p> here are about <a href="https://www.newsmedialists.com/tv/guinea">10 television</a> channels in Guinea.</p>', '<p> here are about nine newspapers in Guinea.</p>', '<p><a href="https://rtgkoloma.info/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Radiodiffusion- elevision Guineenne (RTG)</a></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://rtgkoloma.info/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Radiodiffusion-Television Guineenne (RTG)</a>&nbsp;- state-run, in French, English and vernacular languages; operates Radio Rurale community stations</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.google.com/search?rlz=1C1GCEJ_enGH1021GH1021&amp;cs=0&amp;sca_esv=17b78a64cce673d0&amp;sxsrf=AE3TifOSKmJSnT6RhhIb8Yp3brCF45cBrg%3A1754052700869&amp;q=Horoya&amp;sa=X&amp;ved=2ahUKEwj69YLB0-mOAxUZQEEAHfWQObkQxccNegQIBRAB&amp;mstk=AUtExfBolxWCm3ag9v7mDv32jN3YVz6dxzTf-LtWG8ZptFD-sf8he2bqMjQk4GWALBVS50Jcd_pyjV-q2H3MEmJGpuBBLio82GvmcAM14QBFkvjeRFA25XQksv5aRHaCS4l-4_zkxXstz2WsZHUkVDMT3t4mkfazORznE7h1acJcC_Cv2mtfVTTA1_IowQSOQ75GKHqnCBDHjf11Jme3klL21FY5ZZehGkKe3jhrs8nsk16TUxHPMdC0lmcAckUFgyejC_dPUmv5sQJy7laIu-KfL-mx&amp;csui=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horoya</a> is the state-owned media.</p>', '<p><a href="https://una-oic.org/en/news_agencies/%D9%88%D9%83%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A9-%D8%A3%D9%86%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%A1-%D8%BA%D9%8A%D9%86%D9%8A%D8%A7/">Agence Guineenne de Presse</a> is the state-run news agency.</p>', '<p> he junta in Guinea has put a lot of restrictions on international media in the country, with&nbsp;<a href="https://www.canalplus-afrique.com/gh/">Canal+</a> and &nbsp;pay-TV services platform, <a href="https://m.startimestv.com/browser">StarTimes</a>, ordered by the HAC to withdraw some popular radio and TV channels.</p>', '<p>Social networks such as WhatsApp, Facebook, Instagram and  ikTok are also unavailable without a VPN.</p>', '<p>Internet freedom in Guinea is very limited as the ruling junta <a href="https://pulse.internetsociety.org/en/shutdowns/guinea-enforce-service-blocking-for-second-time-in-2023/">shuts down the internet</a> without explanation and <a href="https://www.africanews.com/2023/05/19/guinea-press-associations-accuse-the-junta-of-censorship/">interferes with same as part of attacks on free expression, including press freedom</a>.</p>', '<p>Horoya, Le Lynx, Le Diplomate,&nbsp; <a href="https://mediaguinee.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MediaGuinee.com</a>, <a href="https://guineenews.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GuineeNews.org</a>, <a href="https://rtgkoloma.info/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Radiodiffusion- elevision Guineenne (RTG)</a>, <a href="https://rtgkoloma.info/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Radiodiffusion-Television Guineenne (RTG)</a>, Radio Nostalgie Guinea, Liberte FM, Soleil FM, Familia FM.</p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2025-12-03 18:45:27.181857'),
(10, 10, '<div>
<h4>Free expression, including press freedom in Liberia is primarily guaranteed by <a href="https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Liberia_1986">the 1986 Constitution</a> &nbsp;through Article 15.&nbsp;Additionally, the <a href="https://natlex.ilo.org/dyn/natlex2/r/natlex/fe/details?p3_isn=113237">Kamara Abdullai Kamara Act of Press Freedom (2019)</a> decriminalized libel, sedition, and criminal malevolence. However, defamation remains a civil offense.</h4>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h4>Liberia also has a <a href="https://www.opengovpartnership.org/members/liberia/commitments/lr0029/">Freedom of Information Act</a> and a <a href="https://web.facebook.com/100066143966818/about/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">National Media Council</a> that promot.es self-regulation among journalists.  he country also has <a href="https://sollib.ug.edu.gh/index.php?p=show_detail&amp;id=32&amp;keywords=">Independent Broadcasting Regulator Act</a>.</h4>
</div>', '<p> he regulatory environment in Liberia is a mix of state oversight and self-regulation by the media itself. The National Media Council, which was created by the Press Union of Liberia (PUL) plays the main oversight role in the media&rsquo;s self-regulation. The <a href="https://www.micat.gov.lr/">Ministry of Information, Culture and Tourism</a>, through its National Communication Bureau (NCB), regulates journalists and journalism, working with professional organizations.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>There is also the <a href="https://www.federalgrants.com/Liberia-Media-Development-LMD-49318.html">Liberia Media Development (LMD) Activity</a>, an agency of the U.S. Agency for International Development (USAID) has funded program aims to increase citizen access to independent and reliable information.</p>', '<p> he <a href="https://web.facebook.com/p/Press-Union-Of-Liberia-Official-100063670865896/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Press Union of Liberia (PUL)</a> is the main journalists&rsquo; Association in Liberia, functioning as an umbrella organization for all journalists in the country.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>There is also <a href="https://web.facebook.com/femalejournals/?locale=tr_TR&amp;_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Female Journalists Association of Liberia (FEJAL)</a> an association of female journalists,</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The Liberia Agricultural Journalists Network (LAJN), a loose association of agricultural journalists, and the</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.aljaonline.org/">Association of Liberian Journalists in the Americas (ALJA)</a>, a US-based organization which focuses on media advocacy, promoting press freedom, and fostering good governance in Liberia through media-related initiative.</p>', '<p>Liberia has is estimated to have some 163 radio stations.</p>', '<p> here are five TV stations &ndash; four privately owned and one state-owned in Liberia.</p>', '<p>Liberia has about 50 newspapers.</p>', '<p> he <a href="https://web.facebook.com/lbsonline/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Liberian Broadcasting System (LBS)</a> is the state-owned broadcaster which operates radio and television outlets. The state also runs the New Liberian, a broadsheet which is published by the Ministry of Information.</p>', '<p>Liberia also has the <a href="https://web.facebook.com/people/Liberia-News-Agency-LINA/61561057540991/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Liberia News Agency (LINA)</a> which is run by the State, and a newswire service, the <a href="https://world.einnews.com/country/liberia">Liberia Newswrire</a>.</p>', '<p>Liberia has at least one well-known international media presence <a href="https://www.ghanaweb.com/GhanaHomePage/SportsArchive/GFA-demands-12-million-from-gov-t-for-re-branding-and-development-956023">BBC World Service</a> and access to content from other international sources through platforms like <a href="https://allafrica.com/liberia/">AllAfrica.com</a>.&nbsp;  he BBC has transmissions available in Liberia, specifically on 103 FM. There is also UNIMIL Radio, a channel run by the United Nations Mission in Liberia.</p>', '<p>Liberia has a constantly growing online media outlets with Facebook as the most availed media platform.</p>', '<p>Internet freedom is challenged.  he government has given cause for concern with potential censorship and surveillance.&nbsp;However, deliberate internet disruption by the government is not known.</p>', '<p><a href="http://www.thenewdawnliberia.com/"> he New Dawn,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.liberianobserver.com/">Daily Observer,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.frontpageafricaonline.com/">FrontPage Africa,&nbsp;</a>Clar TV &ndash; private, Power TV , DC TV, Real TV, Liberian Broadcasting System (LBS) radio, Liberian Broadcasting System (LBS), <a href="http://unmil.unmissions.org/">UNMIL Radio,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.elwaministries.org/">ELWA,</a> Sky FM. &nbsp;</p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2025-11-18 16:40:39.861315'),
(11, 11, '<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"> he </span><a href="https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Mali_1992"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">1992 Constitution</span></a><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"> is the main guarantor of media freedom in Mali. It does this specifically through Part I of Article 4.<span style="mso-spacerun: yes;">&nbsp; </span>Then there is a plethora of ancillary legislative and regulatory mechanisms that provide for other media instruments and functionalities. These <span style="mso-spacerun: yes;">&nbsp;</span>include Law No 018 of 2015 amending and ratifying Ordinance 006 of 2014 establishing the </span><a href="https://www.hac.ml/"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">High Authority for Communication</span></a><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">; Law No 2015-009 of 5 March 2015 on the establishment of the Higher School of Journalism and Communication Sciences, and Decree No 2016-0713/P-RM which set the specifications for private commercial television broadcasting services.</span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"><span style="mso-spacerun: yes;">&nbsp;</span>Decree No 2016-0626/P-RM determines the conditions for the implementation of non-criminal sanctions pronounced by the High Authority of Communication; Decree No 2016-0586/PM-RM of 12 August, sets the specifications for the private services of Commercial Sound Broadcasting; Order No 2017-3038/MESRS-SG of 14 September, 2017 set the conditions of access and the study regime of the&nbsp;<em>Ecole sup&eacute;rieure de journalisme et des sciences de la communication</em>&nbsp;(School of Journalism and Communication Sciences - ESJSC); Decree No 2017-0767/P-RM of 7 September, 2017 determining the products subject to the TV radio license and the methods of indexing and recovery.</span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Decree No 2017-0554/P-RM established the Press Card; Interministerial Order No 2017-1274/MEF-MENUC amended the Interministerial Order No 0442/MENC-MEF-SG, setting the amount and terms of payment of the annual fee for the operation of private audio-visual communication services; Decree No 2016-0713/P-RM set the specifications for private commercial television broadcasting services; while Decree No 2016-0626/P-RM, determines the conditions for the implementation of non-criminal sanctions pronounced by the HAC.</span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Decree No 2017-0767/P-RM determines the products subject to the TV radio license and the methods of indexing and recovery; Law No 00-46/AN-RM of July 2000 regulates offenses related to freedom of expression and the press. This law limits freedom of expression when the media incites racial, national, religious and murder hatred.</span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal" style="tab-stops: list .5in;"><span style="mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Mali criminalizes defamation under Article 115 of its <strong><a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/sgbv-ihrda.uwazi.io/api/files/1564696018624vjey09d867.pdf">p</a></strong><a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/sgbv-ihrda.uwazi.io/api/files/1564696018624vjey09d867.pdf">enal code</a>, which pertains to the defamation of magistrates. Sentences&nbsp;can carry prison terms of up to one year. It does not have access to information laws.</span></p>', '<p> he Haute Autorit&eacute; de la Communication (High Communication Authority - HAC), created by Law No 18 of 2015 is the main regulatory body for the media in Mali. It is mandated to ensure professionalism and responsibility in the press and in communication through the regulation of audiovisual, radio, print and online press. As part of the implementation of its regulatory mission, the HAC has a Media Monitoring and Control Centre.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The&nbsp;Comit&eacute; National de l&rsquo;&Eacute;gal Acc&egrave;s Aux M&eacute;dia d''Etat du Mali&nbsp;(National Committee of Equal Access to State Media - CNEAME) guarantees equal access to state media for all, under the conditions set by the laws and regulations in force. It manages the air time and the editorial space dedicated to candidates and political parties during election campaigns.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The&nbsp;Observatoire pour la D&eacute;ontologie et l&rsquo;&Eacute;thique de la Presse&nbsp;(Observatory of Ethics and Press Ethics - ODEP) is a self-regulating body created in 2001 by journalists. Its impact on media regulation remains very limited because of the lack of self-regulation in Malian media and because initiatives on this subject are unlikely to have any real support from the owners of print outlets.</p>', '<p> here are more than 46 media/journalists&rsquo; associations and professional media networks in Mali. Though independent, these Associations coagulate as the <a href="https://web.facebook.com/MaisonPresseML/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Maison de la Presse</a>, through which they present a common front and also receive state funding for the operation of a Secretariat.</p>
<p>The most relevant are:</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Union Nationale des Journalistes du Mali&nbsp;(National Union of Journalists of Mali - UNAJOM). The first association created in Mali, which is also a member of the West African Journalists Association (WAJA);</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des Journalistes pour la Promotion du Professionnalisme</span><span lang="FR">&nbsp;(Association of Journalists for the Promotion of Professionalism - AJPP);</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des Professionnelles Africaines de la Communication du Mali</span><span lang="FR">&nbsp;(Association of African Communication Professionals of Mali - APAC/Mali);</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Union Internationale de la Presse</span><span lang="FR">&nbsp;Francophone/Mali</span><span lang="FR">&nbsp;(International Union of Francophone Press - UIPF/Mali);</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des Journalistes Sportifs du Mali</span><span lang="FR">&nbsp;(Association of Sports Journalists of Mali - AJSM);</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Organisation des Jeunes Reporters du Mali</span><span lang="FR">&nbsp;(Organisation of Young Reporters of Mali - OJRM).</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Press owners associations:</span></p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des Editeurs de Presse Priv&eacute;e (Association of Publishers of the Private Press - ASSEP);</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des Professionnels de la Presse en Ligne (Association of Online Press Professionals - APPEL Mali);</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Union des Radiodiffusions et T&eacute;l&eacute;visions Libres du Mali (Union of Free Radio and Television of Mali - URTEL);</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">&nbsp;Conseil d&rsquo;&eacute;thiques et de d&eacute;ontologie des pairs (Council of ethics and Professional conduct of peers) CEDEP;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Groupe Patronal de la Presse (Employer'' group of the Press);</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Mutuelle G&eacute;n&eacute;rale de la Solidarit&eacute; de la Presse (General Mutual Fund for Press Solidarity) MUGESPRESSE;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Cadre de Concertation des Directeurs de Publication (Publication Directors'' Consultation Framework) CCDP;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Initiative d&rsquo;Information, de Communication et de Plaidoyer sur la protection Sociale au Mali (Information, Communication and Advocacy Initiative on Social Protection in Mali) ICP-Mali;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Union Internationale de la Presse Francophonie/Mali (International Union of the Francophonie Press / Mali) UIPF/Mali;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des Journalistes pour la Paix (Association of Journalists for Peace) AJPV;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">R&eacute;seau des Communicateurs Maliens pour la lutte contre le Trafic des Enfants (Network of Malian Communicators for the fight against Child Trafficking) RCMLTE.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">R&eacute;seau des Journaliste Economiques du Mali (Network of Economic Journalists of Mali) RJEM;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">R&eacute;seau Malien de Journalistes contre la Pauvret&eacute; et la Corruption (Malian Network of Journalists against Poverty and Corruption) RMJLCP.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Institut pour la D&eacute;mocratie et l&rsquo;Education aux M&eacute;dias (Institute for Democracy and Media Education) IDEM.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Coop&eacute;rative de l&rsquo;habitat des Journalistes du Mali (Housing Cooperative for Journalists of Mali) COHAJOM.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Coordination des Techniciens de la Presse Ecrite (Coordination of Press Technicians) COTEPRESSE.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">R&eacute;seau des Journalistes pour la Promotion du Tourisme au Mali (Network of Journalists for the Promotion of Tourism in Mali) RJMAT.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">R&eacute;seau des Reporters Culturels (Network of Cultural Reporters) RCSF; R&eacute;seau des journalistes pour l&rsquo;action Humanitaire (Network of journalists for Humanitarian action) RJAH- Mali.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des Jeunes Journalistes du Mali (Association of Young Journalists of Mali) AJJM;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Union des Femmes Reporters Sportives d&rsquo;Afrique (Union of Women Sports Reporters of Africa) UFRASA-Mali;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Alliance des Patronnes de la Presse Ecrite (Alliance of Written Press Patrons) APPEM;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des jeunes Reporters du Mali (Association of Young Reporters from Mali) AJRESMA;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Journaliste Citoyen Action pour la R&eacute;publique (Citizen Journalist Action for the Republic) JCAR;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">R&eacute;seau des Journalistes Pour la Promotion de la Paix (Network of Journalists for the Promotion of Peace) RJPP;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des chaines du C&acirc;ble et du Satellite (Association of Cable and Satellite Channels) ACCES)</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">R&eacute;seau des Journalistes pour la s&eacute;curit&eacute; , la paix et le d&eacute;veloppement en Afrique de l&rsquo;ouest (Network of Journalists for Security, Peace and Development in West Africa-) RJPDAO;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Mouvement de Protection de la presse contre les violences (Press Protection Against Violence Movement) ; MPV-Mali</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Union Nationale des Jeunes Editeurs de presse (National Union of Young Press Publishers) UNAJEP;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">R&eacute;seau des Journalistes du secteur Minier (Network of Journalists in the Mining Sector) RJSM</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des Femmes de la Presse du Mali (Association of Women of the Press of Mali) AFPM</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association Devoir de Journaliste &ndash; Mali (Journalist Duty Association &ndash; Mali) RJEAM;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Syndicat du Journalisme Ind&eacute;pendant au Mali (Independent Journalism Union in Mali) SYJIM;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Alliance des Journalistes Pour le D&eacute;veloppement de la Culture (Alliance of Journalists for the Development of Culture) AJDEC;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">R&eacute;seau M&eacute;dia et Droits de l&rsquo;Homme (Media and Human Rights Network) RMDH;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Union des clubs de la Presse Francophone (Union of French Press Clubs) UCPF/Mali.</p>', '<p>According to the 2019&nbsp;Annuaire de M&eacute;dias Mali&nbsp;(Malian Media Yearbook), there are 373 radios in the country.  he government has one national channel and nine regional channels broadcasting in eight regions in addition to Bamako. &nbsp;Moreover, the ministry of youth and citizenship has a network of 11 radios called &ldquo;Voix des jeunes&rdquo;.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Besides commercial radios, there are also community radios and religious ones which broadcast sermons and religious debates.</p>', '<p>According to data collected by HAC in 2019 there are 23 television channels in the country.</p>', '<p>While there are over 300 officially registered newspapers in Mali, only about 30 are actively publishing on a regular basis.</p>', '<p> he&nbsp;<a href="https://www.ortm.ml/radiochaine2">Office de radiodiffusion et de t&eacute;l&eacute;vision du Mali,</a> (<a href="https://www.ortm.ml/">ORTM</a>), is the national broadcaster. It operates ORTM T&eacute;l&eacute;vision nationale, the state-owned TV and national radio broadcasters, <a href="https://www.ortm.ml/radiomali">Radio Nationale</a> and <a href="https://www.ortm.ml/radiochaine2">Chaine 2</a>.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://essor.ml/">L&rsquo;Essor</a> is the state-owned national daily newspaper published in Bamako, the national capital, and distrbuted across the country.</p>', '<p> he&nbsp;<a href="https://amap.ml/">Agence de Presse du Mali&nbsp;(Malian Press Agency - AMAP)</a> is the only agency that redistributes national news to local media.</p>', '<p>International media outlets in Mali include <a title=" V5MONDE" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TV5MONDE">TV5MONDE</a>, FRANCE24, RFI the French channels broadcasting to the countries of&nbsp;<a title="La francophonie" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_francophonie">la francophonie</a>&nbsp;(French-speaking countries worldwide), through satellite transmission.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="http://www.africabletelevision.com/1/">Africable</a>, a free-to-air French Pan African television TV channel is also accessible in Mali.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>It is also possible to subscribe to&nbsp;<a title="CanalSat" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CanalSat">CanalSat</a>&nbsp;Horizons an African channel accessible through subscribe via live satellite reception (RDS, different from MMDS). This way it is possible to watch a large number of channels from Africa as well as Europe, in English and in Arabic</p>', '<p>Online media in Mali is mainly in the form of digital versions of newspapers, blogs, Facebook pages, You ube, WhatsApp channels and online news portals such as <a href="https://www.maliweb.net/">Maliweb.net</a>, <a href="https://malijet.com/">Malijet.net</a>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<a href="https://bamada.net/">Bamada.net</a></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The most visited are&nbsp;<a href="https://www.malipages.com/">Malipages</a>,&nbsp;<a href="https://anpetogo.org/">Agence Nationale Pour l''Emploi&nbsp;</a>(National Employment Agency - ANPE) L&rsquo;Essor and&nbsp;<a href="https://malibaara.com/">Malibaara</a>.</p>', '<p>Internet freedom in Mali has faced significant restrictions, particularly following the 2020 military coup.&nbsp;Access to information has been partially controlled, with website blocks and <a href="https://www.africanews.com/2018/08/11/mali-disrupts-internet-ahead-of-run-off-group/">disruptions</a> of social media applications reported</p>', '<p>OR M occupies a quasi-monopolistic position as the only national transmitter, followed by&nbsp;Africable, a Pan-African channel which does not specifically target the Malian public. Other popular media are Libert&eacute; T&eacute;l&eacute;. Mousso TV,&nbsp;TM1 TV,&nbsp;Energie TV,&nbsp;ETV - Afrique,&nbsp;Diafounou TV,&nbsp;Damb&eacute; TV,&nbsp;Anwar TV, 9 TV, Zahara TV,&nbsp;Diafou TV,&nbsp;Tropic TV,&nbsp;Tafing TV,&nbsp;K&eacute;n&eacute; TV, S&eacute;gou TV, MAICHA, RENOUVEAU TV, M7 TV, CHERIFILA TV NIETA TV, MANDE TV, HONDOU, JOLIBA TV, TROPIC TV, BELEDOUGOU TV, STM and 3 non-commercial (FTN TV&nbsp;and&nbsp;Horonya TV&nbsp;in Bamako and&nbsp;Alafia TV&nbsp;in Gao).</p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2025-11-24 16:47:47.987466'),
(12, 12, '<p> he <a href="https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Mauritania_2012">1991 Constitution</a> guarantees freedom of opinion which essentially includes press freedom, specifically through Article 10. However, the scope of the freedom given is heavily curtailed, due to restrictive laws which constrain the media to heavily self-censor. Of particular pertinence is Article 306 which reads: &lsquo;Anyone who commits an act that violates modesty or Islamic values or who violates any of the sanctities of God, or who helps to do so, and this act is not a crime of hudud [refers to crimes whose punishment is mandated by God], qisas [refers to a crime of revenge], or diyah [refers to a crime requiring compensation paid to the victim], shall be punished through tazir [i.e., at the discretion of the judge] by imprisonment of 3 months to 2 years and by a fine of 50,000 ouguiyas to 600,000 ouguiyas.&rsquo;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Mauritania&rsquo;s </span><strong data-start="465" data-end="518"><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: ''Times New Roman''; mso-fareast-theme-font: major-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"><a href="https://www.refworld.org/legal/legislation/natlegbod/1983/fr/63518"><span style="mso-fareast-font-family: ''Times New Roman''; font-weight: normal;">Penal Code</span></a></span></strong><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"> </span></strong><strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; font-weight: normal;">still provides for criminal defamation</span></strong><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">: Article 348 (criminal code) can be used to prosecute defamation with penalties ranging up <em>to </em><em data-start="627" data-end="663"><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; font-style: normal;">six months to five years in priso</span></em><em>n</em>. Authorities have used this to charge bloggers and others for allegedly spreading false information or defaming public figures.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p data-start="831" data-end="864"><span lang="FR" style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-ansi-language: FR;">Loi n&deg; 2020-015 relative &agrave; la lutte contre la manipulation de l&rsquo;information, criminalizes &ldquo;false news,&rdquo; with prison terms and fines possible.</span></p>
<p data-start="831" data-end="864">&nbsp;</p>
<p data-start="1051" data-end="1078"><span style="font-family: ''Calibri'',sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">The 2010 Press Law technically removed prison sentences for some press offences and limited certain press sanctions to fines, but <em data-start="1211" data-end="1296">prosecutors sometimes still rely on the Penal Code&rsquo;s criminal defamation provisions</em> rather than the press law.</span></p>', '<p><span lang="FR"> he </span><a href="https://www.hapa.mr/fr/"><span lang="FR">Haute Autorit&eacute; de la Presse et de l''Audiovisuel HAPA)</span> <span lang="FR">&nbsp;</span></a><span lang="FR">is Mauritania&rsquo;s official media regulator.</span></p>', '<p>Union of Mauritanian Journalists (UMJ) promotes freedom in Mauritania.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des Journalistes de Mauritanie (AJM) </span><span lang="FR">is the main journalists&rsquo; association in Mauritania.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://web.facebook.com/profile.php?id=100069741345763">Regroupement de la Presse Mauritanienne (RPM)</a> includes 32 organizations from various media outlets. It focuses on promoting freedom of expression and enhancing the capacity of media professionals.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Mauritanian Sport Journalists Association is an association of sports journalists.</p>', '<p> here are about 30 radio stations in Mauritania with at least 15 of them in the national capital, Nouakchott.</p>', '<p> here are 5 known television stations in Mauritania.</p>', '<p> here are some 9 newspapers in Mauritania.</p>', '<p><a href="https://www.ami.mr/Free-Chaab">Chaab,</a> is the state-run daily newspaper, in Arabic.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="http://www.tvm.mr/"> elevision de Mauritanie (TVM),</a> is the state-run television channel. it operates Elmouritania and Elmouritania 2 and thematic networks.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="http://www.radiomauritanie.mr/">Radio Mauritanie,</a> is the state-run radio station. It programmes in Arabic and French, and also operates youth network and Koran network.</p>', '<p><a href="https://www.ami.mr/">Mauritanian News Agency (AMI)</a> is the state-run news agency.</p>', '<p><a title="Radio France Internationale" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radio_France_Internationale">Radio France Internationale</a> (RFI) operates in the country.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a title="BBC World Service" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC_World_Service">BBC World Service</a>, operates FM 106.9 in&nbsp;<a title="Nouakchott" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nouakchott">Nouakchott</a>&nbsp;and 102.4 in&nbsp;<a title="Nouadhibou" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nouadhibou">Nouadhibou</a>.</p>', '<p>Dozens of online media, and <a href="https://gs.statcounter.com/social-media-stats/all/mauritania">social media</a> outlets have emerged in recent times as the State&rsquo;s monopoly on media keeps slipping.</p>', '<p> he state of internet freedom in Mauritania is critical. The government has a history of shutting down the internet, particularly during periods of political instability or protests and even academic activities including <a href="https://mfwa.org/country-highlights/mauritania-shuts-internet-during-national-exams/">examination</a>.&nbsp;In 2023, <a href="https://www.accessnow.org/press-release/internet-shutdown-in-mauritania-stop-the-abuse-turn-it-on/">the government abruptly shut down the internet because there was a prisonbreak</a>. These shutdowns are often implemented during elections, protests, or following events like prison escapes.&nbsp;</p>', '<p><a href="https://www.ami.mr/Free-Chaab">Chaab,&nbsp;</a><a href="https://fr.ami.mr/Free-Horizons">Horizons,&nbsp;</a> <a href="https://www.alakhbar.info/">Akhbar,</a> <a href="http://www.essirage.net/">Essirage,</a> <a href="http://www.lauthentic.info/">L''Authentique,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.tvm.mr/"> elevision de Mauritanie (TVM),&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.saheltv.mr/">Sahel TV,&nbsp;</a> <a href="http://elmourabiton.tv/">Al-Mourabitoun TV,&nbsp;</a>Chinguett TV, <a href="https://www.facebook.com/alwataniyatv.mr/">El Wataniya,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.radiomauritanie.mr/">Radio Mauritanie,&nbsp;</a>Radio Sahara Media FM, Mauritanid FM, <a href="https://www.ami.mr/">Mauritanian News Agency (AMI),&nbsp;</a><a href="https://arriyada.net/">Arriyada,&nbsp;</a><a href="https://www.saharamedias.net/">Sahara Medias,</a> <a href="http://atlasinfo.info/">Atlas Info,&nbsp;</a><a href="https://www.tig.mr/">Tiguend,&nbsp;</a> <a href="https://cridem.org/">Cridem.&nbsp;</a></p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2026-01-29 13:19:05.517295'),
(13, 13, '<p> he primary guarantor of media freedom in Niger is the <a href="https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Niger_2017">2010 Constitution</a>, specifically, through Article 23 which protects freedom of thought, opinion, and expression.&nbsp;Additionally, Niger has a press law; <a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/www.csc-niger.ne/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/ORDONNANCE-N%C2%B0-2010-035-regime-de-liberte-1.pdf">Ordinance N 2010-35</a>, which eliminates prison sentences for press offenses.&nbsp;However, since 2014, the country has had a cybercrime law <a href="https://mfwa.org/issues-in-focus/niger-reintroduces-prison-sentences-for-cybercrime/">that reintroduced prison sentences for cyber offences including defamation</a>, leaving Niger&rsquo;s legal environment conflicted on the matter of criminal defamation.</p>', '<p> he media landscape is regulated by two main authorities:</p>
<p><a href="https://arcep.ne/">L''Autorit&eacute; de R&eacute;gulation des Communications Electroniques et de la Poste, (ARCEP</a>) which ensures that communication services, including those related to media, meet established standards</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Autorit&eacute; de R&eacute;gulation des T&eacute;l&eacute;communications et de La Poste du Niger, which oversees the broader telecommunications sector, including media-related technologies and services.</span></p>', '<p><a href="https://www.medianiger.info/Index.asp?affiche=Photo1.asp">Maison de la Presse du Niger</a> is an umbrella body of journalist associations in Niger and the most prominent of all journalists associations in the country</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://web.facebook.com/assocontrechamps/?locale=fr_FR&amp;_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Association Contrechamps Niger&nbsp;</a>is made up of a group of journalists with the aim to share skills with the southern media landscape.</p>
<p>Association des Journalistes du Niger&nbsp;(AJN) is an association of journalists from the private sector and from public service. It aims is to promote media development.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Association des Promoteurs des Radios Priv&eacute;es du Niger&nbsp;(APRPN) is an association of radio owners. Its aims at improving the conditions for the existence of radios in Niger.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association des Radios et  &eacute;l&eacute;visions Ind&eacute;pendantes&nbsp;</span><span lang="FR">(ARTI) </span><span lang="FR">is an association of both radio and television owners.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">Association Nig&eacute;rienne des &Eacute;diteurs de la Presse Ind&eacute;pendante&nbsp;</span><span lang="FR">(ANEPI) </span><span lang="FR">and Union Nationale des &Eacute;diteurs de la Presse&nbsp;(UNEP) are aasociations of media editors &nbsp;</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://web.facebook.com/CNRacomNiger/?locale=fr_FR&amp;_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Coordination Nationale des Radios Communautaires&nbsp;(CN/RACOM)</a> is the entity federating the community radio stations broadcasting in rural areas.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://web.facebook.com/profile.php?id=100092294565408"><span lang="FR">R&eacute;seau des Journalistes pour les Droits de l&rsquo;Homme</span><span lang="FR">&nbsp;(RJDH)</span></a> <span lang="FR">is a journalists&rsquo; association that promotes human rights</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span lang="FR">The Niger branch of the&nbsp;</span><a href="https://apacniger.org/"><span lang="FR">Association des Professionnelles Africaines de la Communication&nbsp;</span><span lang="FR">(APAC Niger)</span></a> <span lang="FR">is an association of women journalists in Niger. </span>Its aims and objectives are to promote women in general, and women in media in particular.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The Niger branch of the&nbsp;<a href="http://www.presse-francophone.org/">Union de la Presse Francophone&nbsp;(UPF)</a> is part of an international francophone press association which aims to promote the French language and media in French-speaking areas.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>', '<p> here are some 267 radio stations: 67 privately owned and 200 community or association-owned, in Niger.</p>', '<p> here are some 15 privately owned television channels in Niger, in addition to the public television channel, T&eacute;l&eacute; Sahel.</p>', '<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p> here are approximately 12 private French-language weekly or monthly newspapers, some of which are affiliated loosely with political parties.</p>', '<p><a title="Le Sahel (page does not exist)" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Le_Sahel&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1">Le Sahel</a>&nbsp;[<a title="de:Le Sahel" href="https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Sahel">de</a>], and its weekend edition, is the government published national newspaper.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p> he state broadcaster, <a title="ORTN" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ORTN">ORTN</a> runs the national television <a href="http://www.ortn.ne/">Tele-Sahel,</a> the national television channel, and <a href="https://www.ortn.ne/voix-sahel/">Radio Voix du Sahel,</a> the national radio.</p>', '<p><a href="https://www.anp.ne/">Agence Nig&eacute;rienne de Presse&nbsp;</a>(ANP) is the state-run press agency. It issues press releases on matters of security, economy, politics, agriculture, health and sports and culture</p>', '<p><a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/where/niger/">Al Jazeera</a>, has a dedicated page for Niger</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.rfi.fr/fr/podcasts/le-grand-invit%C3%A9-afrique/20250725-au-niger-le-risque-le-plus-important-pour-la-junte-est-la-grogne-sociale-selon-seidik-abba">Radio France Internationale (RFI),</a> and France 24. However, some international broadcasters like RFI and <a href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20241016-niger-renames-its-historic-places-to-sever-ties-with-french-colonial-past">France 24</a> have been suspended by the Nigerien junta.&nbsp;</p>', '<p>Online media in Niger is mainly made up of portals which have dedicated themselves to breaking news and updates, and media by diasporan Nigeriens which also publish in the country.</p>', '<p> he state of internet freedom in Niger is limited by the limitations to access and digital laws that criminalize defamation through electronic media. Also, the government has broad surveillance powers, that include the ability to compel telecommunications companies to assist in intercepting communications</p>', '<p><a href="https://lenqueteur-niger.com/"><span lang="FR">L''Enqueteur,</span></a> <a href="http://www.lesahel.org/"><span lang="FR">Le Sahel,</span></a> <a href="http://nigerexpress.info/"><span lang="FR">Niger Express,</span></a> <a href="https://www.journalduniger.com/"><span lang="FR">Journal du Niger,</span></a> <a href="http://www.tamtaminfo.com/"><span lang="FR"> amtam Info,</span></a> <a href="http://www.actuniger.com/"><span lang="FR">ActuNiger,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://www.niameyetles2jours.com/"><span lang="FR">Niamey et les 2 Jours,</span></a> <a href="https://www.nigerdiaspora.net/accueil"><span lang="FR">Niger, Diaspora,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://sahelien.com/"><span lang="FR">Sahelien,</span></a> <span lang="FR">IciNiger</span><span lang="FR">,&nbsp;Actuniger,&nbsp;Nigerinter&nbsp;and&nbsp;NigerD&eacute;p&ecirc;ches</span><span lang="FR">, </span><span lang="FR">&nbsp;Nigerdiaspora</span><span lang="FR">,</span><span lang="FR">&nbsp;Tamtaminfo, </span><a href="http://www.ortn.ne/"><span lang="FR">Tele-Sahel,</span></a> <a href="http://www.africabletelevision.com/1/"><span lang="FR">Africable TV,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://rttniger.com/"><span lang="FR">Radio-Television Tenere (RTT TV),</span></a> <a href="http://www.ortn.ne/"><span lang="FR">La Voix du Sahel,&nbsp;external</span></a><span lang="FR">, </span><span lang="FR">Radio Saraounia</span><span lang="FR">, </span><span lang="FR">Anfani FM</span><span lang="FR">, </span><span lang="FR">R&amp;M (Radio et Musique)</span><span lang="FR">, </span><a href="https://rttniger.com/"><span lang="FR">Tenere FM,&nbsp;external</span></a><span lang="FR">, </span><a href="http://www.anp.ne/"><span lang="FR">Agence Nigerien de Presse (ANP),</span></a></p>
<p><span lang="FR">&nbsp;</span></p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2025-11-19 11:10:12.721351'),
(14, 14, '<p class="MsoNormal"><a href="https://nigerian-constitution.com/chapter-4-section-39-right-to-freedom-of-expression-and-the-press/"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Chapter 4, Section 39 of the Nigerian constitution</span></a><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"> </span><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">guarantees both free expression and<strong> </strong>freedom of the press.  he country&rsquo;s<strong> </strong></span><a href="https://ncdmb.gov.ng/FOIPolicy.pdf"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Freedom of Information Act, 2011</span></a><strong><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"> </span></strong><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">provisions for the public&rsquo;s right to information. Nigeria directly harbors criminal defamation in its statute through<strong> </strong><span style="color: #040c28;">Section 375 of the </span></span><a href="https://lawsofnigeria.placng.org/laws/C38.pdf"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Criminal Code</span><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"> Act</span></a><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #1f1f1f; background: white;">, which penalizes defamation as a misdemeanor with possible prison terms ranging form 1 to 2 years.</span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">The country&rsquo;s </span><a href="https://cert.gov.ng/ngcert/resources/CyberCrime__Prohibition_Prevention_etc__Act__2024.pdf"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Cybercrime prohibition and prevention Act 2015</span></a><strong><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">,</span></strong><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"> which was amended in 2024, criminalizes false news. Section 373 of the Criminal Code criminalizes defamation, same as Section 391 of the Nigeria </span><a href="https://moj.jg.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/PENAL-CODE-LAW-compressed.pdf"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Penal Code</span></a><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">. Section 3.0.2.1 of the </span><strong><a href="https://www.nta.ng/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/1494416213-NBC-Code-6TH-EDITION.pdf"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; font-weight: normal;">Nigeria Broadcasting</span><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"> </span><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; font-weight: normal;">Cod</span><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">e</span></a></strong><strong><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;"> </span></strong><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">prohibits hate speech with Section 3.0.2.2 provisioning for sanctions against media organizations which violate the code.</span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">There is also the </span><a href="https://www.nta.ng/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/1494416213-NBC-Code-6TH-EDITION.pdf"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">Nigeria Broadcasting Code (BC)</span></a><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">, which was issued by NBC in the exercise of its power under the NBC Act. The BC represents the minimum standard for<span style="mso-spacerun: yes;">&nbsp; </span>broadcasting in Nigeria.</span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">Nigeria has also, periodically revived proposals to regulate online information after the Bill was first introduced in 2019. The&nbsp;<strong><a href="https://techpoint.africa/insight/nigerias-social-media-bill/">Protection from Internet Falsehoods and Manipulations Bill</a></strong> (also known as the social media Bill) aims to criminalize the spread of false information online</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">.&nbsp;<strong>Key provisions include </strong>Fines or imprisonment for spreading &ldquo;false statements&rdquo; online, and Government powers to order platforms or ISPs to block content.&nbsp;<em>The bill has been controversial and not fully implemented in its strongest form.</em></p>', '<p class="MsoNormal"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #474747; background: white;"> he<strong> </strong></span><a href="https://new.presscouncil.gov.ng/category/thepress/"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; background: white;">Nigerian Press Council</span></a><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin; color: #474747; background: white;"> is a Parastatal that regulates the press in the country. </span></p>
<p class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">The National Broadcasting Commission (NBC) set up by the<strong> </strong></span><a href="http://lawsofnigeria.placng.org/view2.php?sn=276" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">the National Broadcasting Commission Act</span></a><span style="font-size: 12.0pt; line-height: 107%; mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;">&nbsp;(the NBC Act) regulates the broadcasting sector in Nigeria. </span></p>', '<p>Key journalists associations include;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p> he <a href="https://nuj.ng/">Nigeria Union of Journalists (NUJ), </a>&nbsp;is a trade union for all media practitioners in Nigeria, founded in 1955 to unify journalists, defend press freedom, and advocate for their welfare, their rights, and ethical standards. it operates as an independent organization with state councils and chapels across the country.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="https://ngeditors.org.ng/">Nigerian Guild of Editors (NGE),</a> is a professional association for senior journalists in editorial leadership across Nigeria&rsquo;s media landscape. It brings together editors and top newsroom managers from print, broadcast, online, and news agency organizations to promote ethical journalism and professional standards</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="https://nuj.ng/about-us/about-nawoj/">Nigerian Association of Women Journalists (NAWOJ),</a> is a professional, non-governmental organization dedicated to supporting, advancing, and advocating for women in the journalism and media profession.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="https://swannational.com.ng/">Sports Writers Association of Nigeria (SWAN)</a> is the professional umbrella body for sports journalists and sports media practitioners in Nigeria</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The Newspaper Proprietors'' Association of Nigeria (NPAN) is a professional body and industry association made up of newspaper publishers in Nigeria. It represents the interests of newspaper owners and plays a role in the regulation and coordination of the Nigerian print media industry.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/brand-journalist-association-of-nigeria-bjan/?originalSubdomain=ng">Brand Journalists'' Association of Nigeria (BJAN)</a>, is a professional body in Nigeria made up of journalists and media practitioners who specialize in reporting on brands, marketing, advertising, public relations, and the broader integrated marketing communications (IMC) industry.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>There are also,</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The Association of Nigerian Journalists and Writers of Tourism,</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The Maritime Journalists Association of Nigeria (MAJAN),</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The Nigeria Auto Journalists Association (NAJA), Association of Communication Scholars &amp; Professionals of Nigeria (ACSPN),</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The Coalition of Nigerian Journalists for Social Change (CNJ) and</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The Human Rights Journalists Network Nigeria.&nbsp;</p>', '<p> here <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_radio_stations_in_Nigeria">are over four hundred radio stations</a> in Nigeria.</p>', '<p> he country reportedly has <a href="https://www.ndr.org.ng/with-740-functional-broadcast-stations-in-nigeria-industry-craves-for-reforms/">more than 740 functional television stations</a>.</p>', '<p>Nigeria is estimated to have some 150 newspapers</p>', '<p>As a federal republic, Nigeria has state-owned radio and television stations both on the federal and state levels. On the federal level, there are the <a href="https://nta.ng/">National  elevision Authority (NTA)</a>, the <a href="https://www.nannews.ng/">News Agency of Nigeria (NAN),</a>&nbsp; and the <a href="http://www.radionigeria.gov.ng/">Federal Radio Corporation of Nigeria (FRCN), </a>&nbsp;The federal government however does not have a newspaper. Across the states, there are more than 60 newspapers.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>All 36 states run at least one radio network and a TV station.</p>', '<p> he <a href="https://nannews.ng/about-us/">Nigeria News Agency (NAN)</a> is the state-run news agency in the country.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>', '<p> hese include <a href="https://www.dstv.com/en-ng/">DSTV Nigeria</a> a subsidiary of Multichoice Africa.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.bbc.com/">BBC</a>, <a href="https://edition.cnn.com/">CNN</a>, <a href="https://www.reuters.com/">Reuters</a>, and <a href="https://apnews.com/">Associated Press</a>,&nbsp;<a href="https://www.google.com/search?rlz=1C1GCEJ_enGH1021GH1021&amp;cs=0&amp;sca_esv=1c2c48b035966a68&amp;sxsrf=AE3TifMqmuYZKkyp4bTe5kFMl-2KwyHZDA%3A1755690933570&amp;q=The+New+York+Times&amp;sa=X&amp;ved=2ahUKEwiHxoGzqpmPAxVUXkEAHbmNER8QxccNegQIBRAB&amp;mstk=AUtExfCB25FKI7DTD6ToJkn__a7JRs8iQXwbgoIo7fnOkSyip9tqtgyuNhd0CLrcTjRoDfMB_zyTyfTFcY8dhrq893iiawX41LMEGM23xWlrcjBtwaYqdFPkEJIKlsbOYxWE8hlW_SQsQQd4E8rCp0P5UlGl0IsMWiduy2Q3QSbBmpZyaPq4IUBt8i64qdiYkJrUNeUDP5fD92NoOrTxDC7dOZwJm4sFPMqvi4Fior6OxC_YRRsmEtzUTytdyfNUM0J1u1-bdQr9y5fNiIpLhtOihkyH&amp;csui=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The New York Times</a> and France 24 are among international media organizations with correspondents or bureaus in Nigeria.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>', '<p>Online media form a prominent part of Nigeria&rsquo;s media landscape, mostly made up of websites of legacy media organizations, but with dedicated news websites and news portals also in the mix.</p>', '<p> he state of internet freedom is mixed. While the government has launched initiatives like offering free internet in public places and developing a National Broadband Plan to improve connectivity, the Nigerian Communications Commission (NCC) has regulations that allow for the interception and storage of data from telecommunications companies. Meanwhile, the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.google.com/search?rlz=1C1GCEJ_enGH1021GH1021&amp;cs=0&amp;sca_esv=1c2c48b035966a68&amp;sxsrf=AE3TifNlb_YR_0ojFP7crXplReoyGiKuDw%3A1755691185501&amp;q=Cybercrime+Act&amp;sa=X&amp;ved=2ahUKEwioy5Grq5mPAxUZXUEAHWF-CC8QxccNegQIKxAB&amp;mstk=AUtExfCkOwWg__djVGNzdRnUwt19t5fcwDx97H1uUd3IwQrB36a_PllzvN3GFrI9sIBz7IgedGDMLX1JAc_EqojD8-cyhjYv0xxgoAQqoGgG__E3fjugXjSkDKTrRRXFHEt1zsB5yAoCKjzenuUY0A2HcOz8fqo5I07NsCnvCLUji_PMynmMfCykFQDEKZSd8AhxCyfbpAHTEj9G5_vAi5_K3oVCbmuEZSLl-vh00M8M2Jy_37ZF0dyhxxZzMlbFpxpe0-KNC7zuNRvKLy8qFbiSFFi8y2OOj2uAEH1LKmhleiwlibCte93NNE5_U1r8JfGeJxzDm2A0dAsJ3SrJSRLYWGph1uGoDAflIh8Jjzrmfq6z&amp;csui=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cybercrime Act</a>&nbsp;has been used to arrest bloggers.</p>', '<p><a href="http://guardian.ng/"> he Guardian,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.punchng.com/">The Punch,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://premiumtimesng.com/">Premium Times,&nbsp;</a> <a href="http://independent.ng/">Daily Independent,&nbsp;</a><a href="https://dailytrust.com/">Daily Trust,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.leadership.ng/">Leadership,</a> <a href="http://www.vanguardngr.com/">Vanguard,&nbsp;external</a> , <a href="http://www.thisdaylive.com/">This Day,&nbsp;</a> <a href="http://www.sunnewsonline.com/">The Daily Sun,</a> <a href="https://tell.ng/">Tell,</a>&nbsp;<a href="http://www.nta.ng/">Nigerian Television Authority (NTA),&nbsp;</a> <a href="https://ait.live/">AIT,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://silverbirdtv.com/">Silverbird TV,</a> <a href="http://www.channelstv.com/">Channels TV,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.radionigeria.gov.ng/">Federal Radio Corporation of Nigeria (FRCN),&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.daargroup.com/">Ray Power,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.freedomradionig.com/">Freedom Radio,&nbsp;</a> <a href="https://www.nannews.ng/">News Agency of Nigeria (NAN)</a>.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2026-03-10 11:11:50.442266'),
(15, 15, '<p>Free expression and Press freedom is guaranteed by the <a href="https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Senegal_2016">2001 (rev. 2016) Constitution</a>, through Article 10. However, Article 11 specifies the guarantee for press freedom.  here is a press code - Law No. 14/2017 which governs print, broadcast and online media.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>However, the press code and other laws restrict free expression. &nbsp;Article 192 of the Press Code provides that in &ldquo;exceptional circumstances&rdquo;, authorities, including political appointees at local government level, may seize equipment, suspend or stop broadcasting or provisionally close a media house &ldquo;in order to prevent or put an end to an infringement of security of the State, to territorial integrity, or in cases of incitement to hatred or an appeal to murder.&rdquo; Articles 248 and 254 of the <a href="https://learningpartnership.org/resource/penal-code-senegal-document-french">Penal Code,</a> 1965, proscribe &ldquo;offending&rdquo; the Head of State, making provision for the for the conviction to a fine of&nbsp; between USD 180-2,700, or imprisonment, of any person who, through public broadcasting (radio, television, cinema, press, display or exhibition) or any other means such as writing, images, songs of &ldquo;any technical process of reaching the public), &ldquo;offends&rdquo; the president. Also under the Penal Code is cybercrime law - <a href="https://www.wipo.int/wipolex/en/legislation/details/6226">Loi n&deg; 2008-11</a> which addresses offenses related to information and communication technologies.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><strong><a href="https://learningpartnership.org/resource/penal-code-senegal-document-french">Senegal&rsquo;s Penal Code</a> </strong>(Law No. 65-60 of July 21, 1965) criminalizes libel and defamation. The crime can attract <strong>criminal sanctions</strong> including fines and imprisonment. Criminal libel is provided for under specific articles (e.g., Article 258 in the Penal Code), though exact text would require consulting the official Code itself.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Article 107 of <a href="https://ictpolicyafrica.org/en/document/4wvmzgfvky">Loi n&deg;2011-01</a> which governs the telecommunications sector gives the regulator power to take &ldquo;any appropriate precautionary measures and to provide sanctions&rdquo; against operators who breach &ldquo;national defence, public security, morality or in the event of a serious breach of its obligations which has effects of creating an irreversible situation or clearly infringes a competitor''s financial interests.&rdquo;</p>', '<p><a href="https://cnra.sn/"><span lang="P -BR">Conseil National de R&eacute;gulation de l&rsquo;Audiovisuel (CNRA)</span></a> <span lang="PT-BR">Regulates audiovisual media (TV, radio). On March 3, 2026,&nbsp; the </span><a href="https://afrinz.ru/en/2026/03/senegal-creates-new-media-regulatory-body/">National Assembly adopted a bill establishing the National Media Regulatory Council (CNRM),</a><span lang="PT-BR">&nbsp;to replace the CNRA. CNRM has a more expanded mandate that positions it oversee the entire information ecosystem: print media, online media, digital platforms and content creators, rather than just audiovisual media</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.senegel.org/en/administration/executive-power/ministers/orgdetails/243">Ministry of Communication and Culture</a> Issues licenses, oversees media policy.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://web.facebook.com/coredsenegal/about/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">CORED (Council for the Respect of Ethics and Deontology)</a> A self-regulatory body that monitors ethical standards in journalism.</p>', '<p><a href="https://web.facebook.com/synpics.senegal/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr"><span lang="FR">Syndicat des Professionnels de l''Information et de la Communication du Senegal (SYNPICS)</span></a> <span lang="FR">is</span><span lang="FR"> a professional union for information and communication professionals.</span></p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://web.facebook.com/p/West-African-Journalists-Association-100063478763880/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">West African Journalists Association (WAJA),</a> an umbrella association for ECOWAS journalists associations, is based in Dakar, Senegal.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Additionally, the <a href="https://www.ifj.org/">International Federation of Journalists (IFJ)</a> has an Africa office in Dakar, actively involved in supporting journalists'' rights and promoting quality journalism.</p>', '<p><a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/www.mfwa.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/11/Senegal- he-Media-and-Participatory-Governance.pdf">There are some 276 radio stations, including 222 community radio stations, 48 commercial stations, and 6 international stations</a>.</p>', '<p> here are some 48 TV stations and web channels.</p>', '<p>Senegal has some 45 newspapers.</p>', '<p><a href="http://www.lesoleil.sn/">Le Soleil,&nbsp;external</a>, is the state-run national daily.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="http://www.rts.sn/">Radiodiffusion  elevision Senegalaise,</a>&nbsp;(RTS) - state-run TV broadcaster, operates RTS1 and RTS2.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="http://www.rts.sn/">Radiodiffusion Television Senegalaise</a>&nbsp;(RTS) - state-run radio broadcaster, operates Chaine Nationale and Senegal Internationale, Dakar FM, regional services.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>', '<p><a href="http://www.aps.sn/">Agence de Presse Senegalaise,</a> is the national state agency.</p>
<p>.</p>', '<p class="MsoNormal"> he<strong> </strong><a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/world-africa-14093758">BBC World Service</a><strong>&nbsp;</strong>is available on FM in Dakar.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">So is<strong> </strong><a href="https://www.rfi.fr/fr/tag/s%C3%A9n%C3%A9gal/">Radio France Internationale</a><strong> </strong>(<strong>RFI</strong>).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a href="https://information.tv5monde.com/pays/senegal"><strong>TV5</strong></a><strong><span style="color: red;"> </span></strong></p>
<p class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a href="https://www.france24.com/fr/tag/s%C3%A9n%C3%A9gal/">France 24</a></p>', '<p>Online media in Senegal is a mix of the <a href="https://www.w3newspapers.com/senegal/">websites of legacy media</a> and blogs by individual micro bloggers.</p>', '<p>Internet freedom in Senegal is restricted. During times of political instability, the government&nbsp;<a href="https://mfwa.org/country-highlights/senegal-suspends-mobile-internet-again-amid-repression-of-protest/">shuts down the internet and blocks access to social media</a>.&nbsp;Also, the government has often shut down the internet with the excuse that it is curbing the spread of mis/disinformation.</p>', '<p><a href="http://www.lesoleil.sn/"><span lang="FR">Le Soleil,&nbsp;</span></a> <a href="https://www.sudquotidien.sn/"><span lang="FR">Sud Quotidien,&nbsp;</span></a> <a href="http://www.lequotidien.sn/"><span lang="FR">Le Quotidien,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://www.nouvelobs.com/"><span lang="FR">L''Observateur,&nbsp;</span></a> <a href="http://www.walf-groupe.com/"><span lang="FR">Wal Fadjri L''Aurore,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="http://www.rts.sn/"><span lang="FR">Radiodiffusion  elevision Senegalaise,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://www.youtube.com/user/tfm"><span lang="FR">TFM,&nbsp;</span></a> <a href="https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCeLEGbj240J6JhpP7ba8GwA"><span lang="FR">2S TV,</span></a> <a href="http://www.rts.sn/"><span lang="FR">Radiodiffusion Television Senegalaise,</span></a> <a href="https://sudfmsenradio.com/"><span lang="FR">Sud FM,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://www.facebook.com/walfadjri.fm/"><span lang="FR">Walf FM,&nbsp;</span></a><a href="https://www.facebook.com/people/Dunyaa-FM-889/100063722803786/"><span lang="FR">Dunyaa FM,&nbsp;</span></a> <a href="http://www.aps.sn/"><span lang="FR">Agence de Presse Senegalaise,&nbsp;</span></a> <a href="http://www.panapress.com/"><span lang="FR">PANA,&nbsp;</span></a></p>
<p><span lang="FR">&nbsp;</span></p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2026-03-10 11:02:52.15072'),
(16, 16, '<p> he <a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/www.sierra-leone.org/Laws/constitution1991.pdf">1991 Constitution </a>&nbsp;guarantees free expression and press freedom specifically, through Section 25.&nbsp;This protection extends to owning and operating media for disseminating information.&nbsp;Sierra Leone also has the <a href="https://sierralii.gov.sl/akn/sl/act/2020/5/eng@2020-08-18">Independent Media Commission (IMC) Act (Act 5) of 2020</a> which <a href="https://mfwa.org/major-boost-for-press-freedom-as-sierra-leone-scraps-criminal-libel-law-after-55-years/">repealed criminal libel</a>.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/www.sierra-leone.org/Laws/2013-02.pdf">Right to Access Information&nbsp; Act, 2013</a> guarantees the public&rsquo;s right to have any information they need, except those subject to exemption, such as information on National security. Under&nbsp; the <a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/www.sierra-leone.org/Laws/1965-46s.pdf">Public Order Act 1965</a>, the country&rsquo;s media regulator, the <a href="https://web.facebook.com/imcsierraleone2000/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Independent Media Commission (IMC)</a> provides an alternative to litigation under the Public Order Act, allowing aggrieved parties to register complaints with the commission, which grants them a hearing. If the IMC agrees that a complaint of defamation or falsehood is valid, it can impose a fine as well as require the offending outlet to publish a retraction and an apology. The IMC can also summon editors at its own discretion.</p>', '<p> he <a href="https://psru.gov.sl/ova_dep/independent-media-commission/">Independent Media Commission (IMC)</a> is the sole media regulator in Sierra Leone. Its members are appointed by the president on the advice of the Sierra Leone Association of Journalists (SLAJ) and subject to the approval of Parliament, according to the Independent Media Commission Act of 2000 as amended in 2006 and 2007.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>While not the regulator, the <a href="https://www.natca.gov.sl/">National Telecommunications Commission (NATCOM)</a> also plays a role in regulating electronic communications, which can intersect with media activities.</p>', '<p> here are&nbsp;several media associations in Sierra Leone, but the most prominent of the lot is the <a href="https://slaj.sl/cgi-sys/suspendedpage.cgi">Sierra Leone Association of Journalists (SLAJ)</a>, which focuses on the welfare and professional development of journalists.&nbsp;MRCG serves as a coordinating body for media reform, research, and capacity building, and includes various stakeholders like SLAJ, the Independent Radio Network, and others.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>There also the <a href="https://mrcgonline.org/">Media Reform Coordinating Group</a> (MRCG) is a coalition of various media organizations and stakeholders working on media reform, research, and capacity building the Independent Radio Network (IRN), which focuses on the interests and development of independent radio stations the Guild of Editors (GoE), Women in the Media Sierra Leone (WIMSAL), which supports and promotes the participation of women in media and the Sierra Leone Reporters Union (SLRU).&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The Guild of Editors (GoE) represents the interests of newspaper editors.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The Sierra Leone Reporters Union (SLRU) focuses on the welfare and rights of journalists, particularly reporters.&nbsp;</p>', '<p> here are about 228 radio stations, including <a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/natcom.gov.sl/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/FM-STATIONS-IN-SIERRA-LEONE.pdf">143 FM stations documented by NATCom</a>.</p>', '<p> here are about 23 TV channels, both state-owned and privately owned, with most operating irregularly.</p>', '<p>Sierra Leone has dozens of newspapers, however <a href="https://ayvnews.com/7-newspapers-registered-170-dead-47-active/">most circulate for a while and go out of production</a>. It is estimated that at least 15 newspapers are in circulation as of 2025 and these are all publishing in Free  own, the national capital, due to high illiteracy rate in the country which is more endemic in the rural areas.</p>', '<p> he <a href="https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCEtHh4-TrhA-4BYgoGyCONQ">Sierra Leone Broadcasting Corporation (SLBC),&nbsp;</a>is the main state-owned media. SLBC operates both as a terrestrial television network and a national radio broadcaster.&nbsp;Though it is state-owned, its coverage is limited.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="https://web.facebook.com/media/set/?vanity=DAILYMAIL.SL&amp;set=a.515038290625699&amp;_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Daily Mail</a>&nbsp; is the State-run newspaper.</p>', '<p> he <a href="https://www.slena.gov.sl/">Sierra Leone News Agency (SLENA)</a> is the state-run news redistributor.</p>', '<p> he&nbsp;<a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/mediaaction/where-we-work/africa/sierra-leone">BBC</a> Media Action, the international development arm of the BBC, works to support media development, improve media freedoms, and produce content on key issues like health, education, and governance&nbsp;.&nbsp;They partner with local media outlets to broadcast programs and provide training and equipment support.</p>', '<p>Online media in Sierra Leone is mainly made up of <a href="https://www.sierra-leone.org/links2.html">websites of legacy media organizations</a>. Online media is only second to radio in terms of patronage.</p>', '<p> hough the government has no track record of shutting down or interrupting the internet, Sierra Leone&rsquo;s Cybersecurity and Crime Act have been criticized for potentially infringing on data protection and privacy rights.</p>', '<p><a href="https://awokonewspaper.sl/">Awoko,</a> <a href="https://slconcordtimes.com/">Concord  imes,</a> <a href="https://www.facebook.com/StandardTiimesPress/">Standard Times,&nbsp;</a><a href="https://thecalabashnewspaper.com/">The Calabash Newspaper,&nbsp;</a><a href="http://www.thesierraleonetelegraph.com/">The Sierra Leone Telegraph,&nbsp;</a><a href="https://slbcnews.webs.com/about-us">Sierra Leone Broadcasting Corporation (SLBC),&nbsp;external</a>, <a href="https://slbcnews.webs.com/about-us">Sierra Leone Broadcasting Corporation (SLBC),&nbsp;</a><a href="http://radiodemocracy.sl/">Radio Democracy,&nbsp;</a>Kiss FM, Skyy Radio, <a href="http://www.capitalradio.sl/">Capital Radio,&nbsp;external</a>, Voice of the Handicapped.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>', '2025-09-08 17:06:38.722429', '2025-11-19 12:19:21.070409'),
(17, 17, '<p> he <a href="chrome-extension://efaidnbmnnnibpcajpcglclefindmkaj/https:/judiciary.gov.gm/sites/default/files/2021-07/Constitution%20of%20The%20Gambia.pdf">1996 Constitution</a> is the primary guarantor of press freedom in Gambia, specifically through Article 207(3). However, this freedom is subject to limitations outlined in Article 209, which allows restrictions in the interests of national security, public order, morality, and the protection of others'' reputations. There is also the revised 2005 Press Law, which prohibits censorship and guarantees the right to obtain information.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="Criminal%20Code%20Act,%201933,">Gambian Criminal code</a> however, criminalizes defamation and false news, and also makes provision for the prosecution of journalists for sedition, even though defamation is already a civil offense.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The&nbsp;<a href="https://www.google.com/search?rlz=1C1GCEJ_enGH1021GH1021&amp;cs=0&amp;sca_esv=764388d8aea36402&amp;sxsrf=AE3TifMxgifPoTCUl9hRTj2g4v9WmeBhNQ%3A1754565853565&amp;q=Gambia%27s+Information+and+Communications+Act&amp;sa=X&amp;ved=2ahUKEwjm2a2Ty_iOAxW-V0EAHQ4GBBcQxccNegQIAxAB&amp;mstk=AUtExfBgeqp8szUFm-TEsxyQ19vf4M14rZEU34GDMZxSiIVREZM0IwnVZOaQtBVmarWGi2CDNXcNjUUc8rkeC6p1tLUdDAc0GVwb72Aa7YcvVFgn8B5110d7U3_WtNtLvAaCpiFnSG7LetaioBOnoMd1v2B6kjkhAJPbMVzmikHH_PGOPwHOH9jafKM0-rkbEN7AZbGOqYIcMo-x5SZOhdxVRQ2gCg2TDGbGUhIVP8X5sO4eZX0MIcsuFETzO0d__F57uvu0beUzAB0N3syRaOaBWqxQ&amp;csui=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener"> Information and Communications Act</a>,&nbsp;officially the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.google.com/search?rlz=1C1GCEJ_enGH1021GH1021&amp;cs=0&amp;sca_esv=764388d8aea36402&amp;sxsrf=AE3TifMxgifPoTCUl9hRTj2g4v9WmeBhNQ%3A1754565853565&amp;q=Information+and+Communications+Act%2C+2009&amp;sa=X&amp;ved=2ahUKEwjm2a2Ty_iOAxW-V0EAHQ4GBBcQxccNegQIAxAC&amp;mstk=AUtExfBgeqp8szUFm-TEsxyQ19vf4M14rZEU34GDMZxSiIVREZM0IwnVZOaQtBVmarWGi2CDNXcNjUUc8rkeC6p1tLUdDAc0GVwb72Aa7YcvVFgn8B5110d7U3_WtNtLvAaCpiFnSG7LetaioBOnoMd1v2B6kjkhAJPbMVzmikHH_PGOPwHOH9jafKM0-rkbEN7AZbGOqYIcMo-x5SZOhdxVRQ2gCg2TDGbGUhIVP8X5sO4eZX0MIcsuFETzO0d__F57uvu0beUzAB0N3syRaOaBWqxQ&amp;csui=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Information and Communications Act, 2009</a>, is a law that regulates the information and communications sectors in The Gambia.</p>', '<p>Gambia Press Union (GPU) actively monitors media ethics, promotes professional standards, and defends journalists'' rights.&nbsp; hey also play a key role in training media practitioners on ethical issues and media monitoring.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Gambia Media Council acts as an independent watchdog for media ethics and compliance, working alongside the GPU.&nbsp;</p>', '<p> he&nbsp;<a href="https://gpu.gm/gambian-media-has-self-regulatory-body/">Gambia Press Union (GPU)</a> is an umbrella organization for media professionals and aims to safeguard their welfare and interests, promote freedom of expression, and enhance media development.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://web.facebook.com/watch/?v=2215684612203804">Sports Journalists'' Association of The Gambia (SJAG)</a> is an Association of Sports journalists.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.google.com/search?rlz=1C1GCEJ_enGH1021GH1021&amp;cs=0&amp;sca_esv=764388d8aea36402&amp;sxsrf=AE3TifOJP7XpjJYPvocH0eqpr7pPNWjpEg%3A1754567914892&amp;q=Women+Journalist+Association+of+The+Gambia+%28WoJAG%29&amp;sa=X&amp;ved=2ahUKEwjJ4Z3q0viOAxXnTkEAHfqGBw4QxccNegQIFRAB&amp;mstk=AUtExfBJZR6mM6jRKS3Mb14Ki_U2XmboXWWIFaHOiO3EIrpzK6AlqqszOHxdNUSbiXAH870JpA_LOdxGP7zcUWjoqF7ZP_ukK3YAwRwc085HAcwzZJ8c-9BlCnoNCA2I8XMFpO2kWqHW0KSIghJx8FVBcItN5rvUDhsMWYvdQTd33sqLYEyqP3lwLmHu2sMsAwH34LGMzGmOb9bV99ZgzlztJ34wCFV8xFB639FdTOgxmacr1p64bU3EWBp-4QurzoD7XSNVlJi9qQAvHigAOOSdk28Q&amp;csui=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Women Journalist Association of The Gambia (WoJAG)</a> is an Association of women journalists in The Gambia. WoJAG focuses on empowering women journalists in The Gambia, particularly in addressing harmful traditional practices and gender discrimination.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="https://www.google.com/search?rlz=1C1GCEJ_enGH1021GH1021&amp;cs=0&amp;sca_esv=764388d8aea36402&amp;sxsrf=AE3TifOJP7XpjJYPvocH0eqpr7pPNWjpEg%3A1754567914892&amp;q=Young+Journalists%27+Association+of+The+Gambia+%28YJAG%29&amp;sa=X&amp;ved=2ahUKEwjJ4Z3q0viOAxXnTkEAHfqGBw4QxccNegQIExAB&amp;mstk=AUtExfBJZR6mM6jRKS3Mb14Ki_U2XmboXWWIFaHOiO3EIrpzK6AlqqszOHxdNUSbiXAH870JpA_LOdxGP7zcUWjoqF7ZP_ukK3YAwRwc085HAcwzZJ8c-9BlCnoNCA2I8XMFpO2kWqHW0KSIghJx8FVBcItN5rvUDhsMWYvdQTd33sqLYEyqP3lwLmHu2sMsAwH34LGMzGmOb9bV99ZgzlztJ34wCFV8xFB639FdTOgxmacr1p64bU3EWBp-4QurzoD7XSNVlJi9qQAvHigAOOSdk28Q&amp;csui=3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Young Journalists'' Association of The Gambia (YJAG)</a> is an association for mostly early career journalists.</p>', '<p>According to a list of broadcast media outlets published by <a href="https://pura.gm/ict/sub-sectors/broadcasting/"> he Gambia Public Utilities Regulatory Authority, there are 47 radio stations comprising 38 commercial radio stations and 9 community 9 community radio channels</a>.</p>', '<p> he Gambia Public Utility Regulatory Authority lists 5 television channels as operational in the country.</p>', '<p> here are about 70 newspapers in The Gambia.</p>', '<p><a href="http://www.grts.gm/">Radio Gambia,&nbsp;external</a>&nbsp;- state broadcaster, programmes in English and vernacular languages.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><a href="http://www.grts.gm/">Gambia  elevision (GRTS TV),</a> is the national television broadcaster.&nbsp;</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The <a href="https://web.facebook.com/gambiadaily/?_rdc=1&amp;_rdr">Gambia Daily News</a> is the state-owned newspaper.</p>', '<p> he <a href="https://www.gamna.gov.gm/about">Gambia News Agency (GAMNA)</a> is the national news redistributor.</p>', '<p>No known international media organizations operate in  he Gambia.</p>', '<p class="MsoNormal">Online media in  he Gambia is an admixture of the<strong> </strong><a href="https://www.accessgambia.com/information/news-media.html"><strong>websites of legacy media</strong></a><strong> </strong>and blogs.</p>', '<p>Since the end of the Yahaya Jameh regime, Internet freedom has improved,&nbsp;with access to previously blocked websites and a platforms opened.&nbsp;However, challenges remain, including the continued use of restrictive laws like sedition and criminal defamation to stifle criticism, and instances of violence and intimidation against journalists.</p>
<p> here is also no credible information that the government is surveying the internet usage. <!-- [if !supportAnnotations]-->&nbsp;</p>
